WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:04.004
Running on vast amounts of real-time data,

00:00:04.004 --> 00:00:06.297
traveling through high-speed networks,

00:00:06.297 --> 00:00:09.551
the cities of the future will
be technologically smart.

00:00:11.094 --> 00:00:14.055
The companies designing
these smart systems say

00:00:14.055 --> 00:00:16.766
they'll make our urban
environments more livable,

00:00:16.766 --> 00:00:18.059
they'll keep traffic flowing,

00:00:18.059 --> 00:00:20.812
save energy and even help keep us safe.

00:00:23.523 --> 00:00:27.110
Countries all across the world
are embracing smart cities,

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:29.529
but none more so than China.

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:32.157
This is China's newest and
most exquisite blueprint

00:00:32.157 --> 00:00:33.491
in the new era

00:00:33.491 --> 00:00:36.036
that presents the image
of a confident, beautiful

00:00:36.036 --> 00:00:38.163
and innovative China.

00:00:38.163 --> 00:00:42.208
This is a concept favored
by Xi Jinping himself,

00:00:42.208 --> 00:00:45.628
the notion of smart
cities to smart society

00:00:45.628 --> 00:00:46.796
and that has become

00:00:46.796 --> 00:00:49.466
a sort of, like for me, a national strategy.

00:00:49.466 --> 00:00:52.594
To sustain its already
high levels of productivity,

00:00:52.594 --> 00:00:56.014
Beijing has pumped an
estimated $800 billion

00:00:56.014 --> 00:00:58.767
into high-speed rail, autonomous vehicles,

00:00:58.767 --> 00:01:01.352
smart grids and 5G networks

00:01:01.352 --> 00:01:05.148
and is piloting more than
half the world's smart cities.

00:01:05.148 --> 00:01:07.525
But for many, the trade-offs are alarming.

00:01:08.526 --> 00:01:10.779
High-tech surveillance
is already widespread

00:01:10.779 --> 00:01:12.322
for Chinese citizens

00:01:12.322 --> 00:01:14.199
and it's a growing concern for countries

00:01:14.199 --> 00:01:17.619
who import these systems
from Chinese tech firms.

00:01:17.619 --> 00:01:20.622
China's exporting elements
of its surveillance complex

00:01:20.622 --> 00:01:21.873
to third countries

00:01:21.873 --> 00:01:24.334
through the export of what
they call smart cities.

00:01:24.334 --> 00:01:27.587
By 2030, China is set to overtake the U.S.

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:29.923
and become the world's number one economy

00:01:29.923 --> 00:01:31.633
and it's betting that smart cities

00:01:31.633 --> 00:01:33.968
will be a key part of that success.

00:01:45.438 --> 00:01:47.524
The story of China's urbanization

00:01:47.524 --> 00:01:50.276
is one without historic parallel.

00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:54.823
Every year 20 new cities are
built from scratch in China.

00:01:54.823 --> 00:01:57.659
More people now live in
China's cities and towns

00:01:57.659 --> 00:01:58.785
than live in its countryside.

00:02:08.002 --> 00:02:10.630
The economic reforms of the 1970s

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:13.716
drove hundreds of millions of
people out of rural poverty

00:02:13.716 --> 00:02:16.678
and into China's cities in search of work.

00:02:16.678 --> 00:02:18.346
Many would end up in the factories

00:02:18.346 --> 00:02:21.558
that powered the nation's
meteoric economic rise.

00:02:34.571 --> 00:02:37.657
That's more than 10% of
the world's population

00:02:37.657 --> 00:02:40.201
living in Chinese cities.

00:02:40.201 --> 00:02:41.786
Such is the pace of development

00:02:41.786 --> 00:02:44.038
it can be hard to get your
head around the numbers.

00:02:44.038 --> 00:02:47.709
China has over 100 cities
each with their own population

00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:49.669
of over a million people.

00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:52.130
Despite this, China's urbanization rate,

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:55.466
the percentage of people in
cities, is still some way behind

00:02:55.466 --> 00:02:59.095
other advanced economies,
like the U.S. and Japan.

00:02:59.095 --> 00:03:03.433
But by 2050, urbanization
is expected to hit 80%

00:03:03.433 --> 00:03:06.394
and it's all part of Beijing's plans.

00:03:38.676 --> 00:03:41.763
That's where smart cities come into it.

00:03:41.763 --> 00:03:45.016
A new smarter urbanization
had already been designated

00:03:45.016 --> 00:03:46.392
a national priority

00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:49.896
in the National New Type
Urbanization Plan in 2014.

00:03:51.439 --> 00:03:54.317
As well as encouraging
permanent migration to cities,

00:03:54.317 --> 00:03:56.361
that plan called for high-speed rail links

00:03:56.361 --> 00:03:58.279
and other infrastructure.

00:03:58.279 --> 00:04:00.406
But to sustain these urban populations,

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:04.035
the cities also have to
be digitally advanced.

00:04:04.035 --> 00:04:06.579
In the Communist Party's
latest five-year plan,

00:04:06.579 --> 00:04:09.499
five interconnected city
clusters will be rolled out

00:04:09.499 --> 00:04:11.501
by the mid-2030s.

00:04:11.501 --> 00:04:13.795
Beijing contends they'll be world leaders

00:04:13.795 --> 00:04:15.838
in smart city infrastructure.

00:04:15.838 --> 00:04:19.717
Each will also be home to as
many as 100 million people.

00:04:19.717 --> 00:04:22.470
Xiongan is designed to relieve Beijing

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:25.848
of functions not essential
to its role as the capital

00:04:25.848 --> 00:04:28.768
and provide a Chinese
solution to urban problems.

00:04:28.768 --> 00:04:30.520
This smart city strategy fits into

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:32.438
the economic transformation

00:04:32.438 --> 00:04:37.235
or the qualitative upgrade
of its economy very well

00:04:37.235 --> 00:04:40.446
because smart city ticks all the boxes;

00:04:40.446 --> 00:04:43.366
innovation, technology, new economy

00:04:43.366 --> 00:04:45.243
and economic upgrade.

00:04:45.243 --> 00:04:48.413
It's a sort of like new urban utopia

00:04:48.413 --> 00:04:50.873
or a new urban imaginary

00:04:50.873 --> 00:04:53.668
that gives the policymakers in China

00:04:53.668 --> 00:04:57.505
some aspirations and some
inspirations as well.

00:04:58.381 --> 00:05:00.675
In the Pearl River Delta
cluster in the south

00:05:00.675 --> 00:05:03.886
is one of the most
advanced cities, Shenzhen.

00:05:03.886 --> 00:05:07.265
China's answer to Silicon
Valley used to be a fishing town

00:05:07.265 --> 00:05:08.850
40 years ago.

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:11.185
As its population grew 40-fold,

00:05:11.185 --> 00:05:15.565
GDP grew 11,452-fold.

00:05:15.565 --> 00:05:18.276
When I entered China in January, 2015,

00:05:18.276 --> 00:05:19.777
the city of Shenzhen, if I'm correct,

00:05:19.777 --> 00:05:22.947
had around 13-point-some million people

00:05:22.947 --> 00:05:23.906
living and working.

00:05:23.906 --> 00:05:25.742
Right now, if we look
at the amount of people

00:05:25.742 --> 00:05:26.951
living and working there now,

00:05:26.951 --> 00:05:31.039
it is close to I think, 18
or 19, maybe even 20 million.

00:05:31.039 --> 00:05:32.665
Headquartered in Shenzhen,

00:05:32.665 --> 00:05:35.585
Huawei is China's biggest tech firm.

00:05:35.585 --> 00:05:38.129
When it comes to intelligent
cities and connected cities,

00:05:38.129 --> 00:05:40.381
we have supported the
majority of the world

00:05:40.381 --> 00:05:43.176
with global and mobile
broadband infrastructures.

00:05:43.176 --> 00:05:46.679
All these digitized technologies
are helping this industry

00:05:46.679 --> 00:05:48.139
to also move higher up the value chain

00:05:48.139 --> 00:05:50.350
in a much shorter timeframe.

00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:54.312
In 2020, Shenzhen deployed
next generation 5G

00:05:54.312 --> 00:05:56.022
and high-speed broadband.

00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:59.025
Huawei, the market leader,
played a major role.

00:05:59.025 --> 00:06:03.112
Huawei has completed a
series of 5G new radio tests.

00:06:03.112 --> 00:06:05.198
The downlink peak throughput has reached

00:06:05.198 --> 00:06:08.326
over six gigabytes per
second for a single user.

00:06:08.326 --> 00:06:10.453
In Shenzhen, average broadband speeds

00:06:10.453 --> 00:06:12.663
are about one gigabyte per second.

00:06:12.663 --> 00:06:14.707
That's given local companies an edge

00:06:14.707 --> 00:06:16.376
when it comes to productivity.

00:06:17.543 --> 00:06:19.253
The timing of smart traffic lights

00:06:19.253 --> 00:06:21.547
are adjusted to improve flow efficiently.

00:06:23.049 --> 00:06:26.386
Surveillance camera systems
capture traffic violations.

00:06:27.762 --> 00:06:29.555
It sits within a horizontal layer

00:06:29.555 --> 00:06:31.849
of mobile and broadband
and fixed technologies

00:06:31.849 --> 00:06:32.975
to connect it all.

00:06:32.975 --> 00:06:34.519
That's what makes it intelligent.

00:06:34.519 --> 00:06:36.354
It understands what it is for.

00:06:36.354 --> 00:06:38.648
It optimizes and it utilizes

00:06:38.648 --> 00:06:41.692
all these broadband
connectivities for applications

00:06:41.692 --> 00:06:43.569
for people to stay at home

00:06:43.569 --> 00:06:46.489
and whilst at home using
applications from the government,

00:06:46.489 --> 00:06:50.326
for example, to do this citizen
or residency registration,

00:06:50.326 --> 00:06:52.870
or transfer from one city to another city.

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:55.665
The same for taxation,
the same for utilities,

00:06:55.665 --> 00:06:58.751
gas, water, electricity
and what have you.

00:06:58.751 --> 00:07:01.295
No more need to go to city hall,

00:07:01.295 --> 00:07:04.590
which is downtown city
center, for paperwork

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:05.758
and what have you.

00:07:05.758 --> 00:07:08.553
In the process of all of this, in return,

00:07:08.553 --> 00:07:10.346
it lowers the burden
on traffic, of course,

00:07:10.346 --> 00:07:12.515
because these millions of people

00:07:12.515 --> 00:07:14.976
in five, six years timeframe

00:07:14.976 --> 00:07:17.812
added to the population of Shenzhen in itself

00:07:17.812 --> 00:07:21.023
would also burden traffic
in a tremendous way.

00:07:21.941 --> 00:07:22.775
But of course,

00:07:22.775 --> 00:07:25.862
these smart cities are imprinted
with the characteristics

00:07:25.862 --> 00:07:28.197
of China's surveillance state.

00:07:28.197 --> 00:07:29.949
The Chinese people are being watched

00:07:29.949 --> 00:07:32.577
in ways that earlier generations were not.

00:08:01.981 --> 00:08:04.859
I think there's a pretty
crucial distinction to make

00:08:04.859 --> 00:08:09.864
in privacy in terms of
protection of one's personal data

00:08:09.864 --> 00:08:13.659
against companies misusing it

00:08:13.659 --> 00:08:17.705
and privacy in the sense of
an intrinsic civil liberty

00:08:17.705 --> 00:08:21.292
to protect against overly
excessive government interference

00:08:21.292 --> 00:08:22.543
into one's personal life,

00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:26.088
Privacy, in the first sense,

00:08:26.088 --> 00:08:29.300
there are actually very
robust discussions in China.

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:32.053
There are personal
information protection laws

00:08:32.053 --> 00:08:35.181
being drafted, data
security laws being drafted

00:08:35.181 --> 00:08:37.642
that are meant to protect privacy

00:08:37.642 --> 00:08:40.228
from company intrusion.

00:08:40.228 --> 00:08:42.980
As for the privacy in the second sense,

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:44.440
there's very little discussion,

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:48.361
very little protection
from government intrusion

00:08:48.361 --> 00:08:50.029
upon one's personal privacy.

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:53.074
If companies are collecting less data

00:08:53.074 --> 00:08:54.742
and are being more secure

00:08:54.742 --> 00:08:57.245
with how they manage your personal data

00:08:57.245 --> 00:08:59.121
and your personal information,

00:08:59.121 --> 00:09:01.415
that might also limit the
ability of the government

00:09:01.415 --> 00:09:03.459
to get data from companies.

00:09:03.459 --> 00:09:06.254
Regarding Chinese citizens' reactions

00:09:06.254 --> 00:09:09.465
to AI intruding upon their personal lives,

00:09:09.465 --> 00:09:13.010
there's a lot more discussion
in Chinese social media,

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:17.348
Chinese blogs, than we
would assume in the West.

00:09:18.849 --> 00:09:20.726
Inside China, local governments

00:09:20.726 --> 00:09:23.646
now have a more effective
way to monitor citizens

00:09:23.646 --> 00:09:25.398
on a real-time basis.

00:09:25.398 --> 00:09:27.400
Under China's Cyber Security Law,

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:30.528
companies must store users'
data on local servers

00:09:30.528 --> 00:09:33.114
and decrypt the data on
request from the authorities.

00:09:33.114 --> 00:09:34.615
A new data security law

00:09:34.615 --> 00:09:37.493
which just passed on June 11th

00:09:37.493 --> 00:09:40.413
is something that Chinese
government really cared about

00:09:40.413 --> 00:09:43.082
and no one is going to get past it.

00:09:43.082 --> 00:09:44.917
Others worry that Chinese companies

00:09:44.917 --> 00:09:47.670
may share sensitive
information picked up abroad

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:50.464
with Chinese intelligence services.

00:09:50.464 --> 00:09:53.384
Huawei has famously been
accused of being dangerous

00:09:53.384 --> 00:09:54.844
because countries like the U.S.

00:09:54.844 --> 00:09:56.846
think it could use its telecom equipment

00:09:56.846 --> 00:09:58.723
to spy for the Chinese government.

00:09:59.849 --> 00:10:01.809
Huawei denies the allegations,

00:10:01.809 --> 00:10:05.646
arguing that U.S. restrictions
aren't about cybersecurity

00:10:05.646 --> 00:10:08.566
but are really designed to
safeguard American dominance

00:10:08.566 --> 00:10:10.276
of global tech.

00:10:10.276 --> 00:10:13.446
China's ambassador to the U.K. tells the BBC

00:10:13.446 --> 00:10:17.366
that Britain's decision to
drop Huawei from its 5G network

00:10:17.366 --> 00:10:19.827
is a bad move for the country.

00:10:19.827 --> 00:10:21.370
I think the U.K. should have

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:23.831
its own independent foreign policy

00:10:23.831 --> 00:10:27.251
rather than to dance to
the tune of Americans.

00:10:28.878 --> 00:10:30.796
Despite the international scrutiny,

00:10:30.796 --> 00:10:32.465
there still seems to be a demand

00:10:32.465 --> 00:10:34.592
for China's smart city model.

00:10:35.426 --> 00:10:37.553
Chinese vendors have won contracts

00:10:37.553 --> 00:10:41.307
for smart city infrastructure
in about 50 cities worldwide

00:10:41.307 --> 00:10:44.727
according to a study by RWR Advisory.

00:10:44.727 --> 00:10:47.855
Most are in illiberal societies.

00:10:47.855 --> 00:10:50.941
Whether such privacy fears
are overstated or not,

00:10:50.941 --> 00:10:52.610
the opportunities are too great

00:10:52.610 --> 00:10:55.196
for China big tech to ignore.

00:10:55.196 --> 00:10:57.740
Chinese CEOs lined up
to pledge their support

00:10:57.740 --> 00:10:59.158
for President Xi Jinping's

00:10:59.158 --> 00:11:01.535
recent "common prosperity" policy

00:11:01.535 --> 00:11:03.704
despite the hit their
businesses would take

00:11:03.704 --> 00:11:06.624
as part of his crackdown
on various industries.

00:11:07.583 --> 00:11:12.755
The regulation of the technology giants

00:11:12.755 --> 00:11:16.258
and to what extent our
behavior, our action,

00:11:16.258 --> 00:11:21.389
even our travel patterns,
our shopping and preferences,

00:11:21.389 --> 00:11:25.101
they are being utilized
for commercial purposes

00:11:25.101 --> 00:11:29.897
and again, this is a global
issue and a global concern.

00:11:29.897 --> 00:11:32.900
They're huge market opportunities.

00:11:32.900 --> 00:11:36.904
So they work together
to promote what I call

00:11:36.904 --> 00:11:38.906
the smart city movement in China.

00:11:40.199 --> 00:11:42.368
But Beijing's dreams of utopian living

00:11:42.368 --> 00:11:44.495
could come crashing down.

00:11:44.495 --> 00:11:47.456
The very urbanization
that's led to smart cities

00:11:47.456 --> 00:11:50.042
also resulted in vast ghost towns

00:11:50.042 --> 00:11:52.128
as the pace of building was too fast

00:11:52.128 --> 00:11:53.754
for newcomers moving in.

00:12:02.012 --> 00:12:03.389
For the developers,

00:12:03.389 --> 00:12:07.101
kick-starting these projects
means taking on huge debt.

00:12:07.101 --> 00:12:09.478
So when buildings aren't
filled quickly enough,

00:12:09.478 --> 00:12:12.565
that can mean companies going bust.

00:12:12.565 --> 00:12:14.316
Chinese authorities are now grappling

00:12:14.316 --> 00:12:16.944
with how to handle developers
like Evergrande Group,

00:12:16.944 --> 00:12:17.820
which now account for

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:19.989
almost half the world's distressed debt.

00:12:21.449 --> 00:12:23.117
If China's economy takes a hit

00:12:23.117 --> 00:12:25.494
while its property developers go bust,

00:12:25.494 --> 00:12:28.414
smart cities suddenly become
a lot harder to build.

00:12:30.416 --> 00:12:32.668
Despite its Orwellian overtones,

00:12:32.668 --> 00:12:35.588
this convergence between the
state and private tech firms

00:12:35.588 --> 00:12:39.925
to create smart city technology
clearly has major benefits.

00:12:39.925 --> 00:12:42.595
Smart city apps helped
China contain the spread

00:12:42.595 --> 00:12:44.305
of the Covid-19 virus,

00:12:44.305 --> 00:12:46.182
allowing Beijing to turn its economy

00:12:46.182 --> 00:12:50.186
from contraction to growth
faster than any other nation

00:12:50.186 --> 00:12:53.647
and smart city ambassadors
like Edwin Diender at Huawei

00:12:53.647 --> 00:12:57.026
see their technologies as
essential to our future.

00:12:57.026 --> 00:12:57.985
It creates more insights

00:12:57.985 --> 00:13:01.280
in terms of how and where
is this city, for example,

00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:04.617
consuming electricity, consuming power

00:13:04.617 --> 00:13:06.202
and only with digital

00:13:06.202 --> 00:13:08.829
we're able to create a
visualization for that,

00:13:08.829 --> 00:13:10.289
create a better understanding for that,

00:13:10.289 --> 00:13:11.332
but most importantly,

00:13:11.332 --> 00:13:13.501
have a principle of big data analytics

00:13:13.501 --> 00:13:15.336
to help us understand and create insights

00:13:15.336 --> 00:13:16.545
in how to anticipate.

00:13:16.545 --> 00:13:18.088
But I think the most important one

00:13:18.088 --> 00:13:20.883
and the one that is the most sustainable,

00:13:20.883 --> 00:13:22.968
of course sits with energy transition.

00:13:22.968 --> 00:13:25.054
I truly believe energy transition

00:13:25.054 --> 00:13:28.015
is the next wave of innovation
for digital transformation.

00:13:42.696 --> 00:13:45.616
The real question is whether
smart cities will be designed

00:13:45.616 --> 00:13:47.034
for the people who live in them

00:13:47.034 --> 00:13:50.621
or for the governments and
tech firms who run them?

00:13:50.621 --> 00:13:52.832
I strongly believe that a smart city,

00:13:52.832 --> 00:13:55.042
even though it's built upon technology,

00:13:55.042 --> 00:13:57.878
but it should not be technology-centric.

00:13:57.878 --> 00:14:01.215
It's about the livability, sustainability

00:14:01.215 --> 00:14:05.052
and the public good and
the social inclusion.

00:14:06.679 --> 00:14:09.014
Our cities will thrive in the future

00:14:09.014 --> 00:14:11.976
by building and upgrading
infrastructure with technologies

00:14:11.976 --> 00:14:13.769
that tackle the biggest challenges

00:14:13.769 --> 00:14:16.063
of a rapidly urbanizing world.

00:14:16.689 --> 00:14:19.567
China aims to be a leader in this field,

00:14:19.567 --> 00:14:21.485
both at home and abroad.

00:14:22.444 --> 00:14:24.196
One thing is for sure,

00:14:24.196 --> 00:14:27.741
China's urban landscape will
be dramatically refashioned

00:14:27.741 --> 00:14:29.118
in the decades ahead.

